course: dictionary
lesson: 10090
title: A thermos keeps things hot or cold
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A thermos is a special bottle, also called a vacuum flask or just a flask, that keeps drinks hot or cold for a long time. You can see it in a sentence about animals: «Arctic animals are built like thermos flasks.» Those animals keep their warmth in, just as a thermos does.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. The sentence lists «thick fur and fat» and animals living «in tunnels under the snow blanket». These are all ways of keeping heat in. So a thermos must be something that keeps heat in, or stops it getting out.
explain: A thermos often holds «a thermos of tea» on a picnic or school trip. Check your meaning by swapping it in: «a bottle that keeps the tea hot» still makes sense, so you have understood the word.
fact: A thermos is a bottle or flask with two walls and a gap between them.
fact: The gap keeps hot drinks hot and cold drinks cold for a long time.
fact: A thermos is also called a vacuum flask or a flask.
fact: The word is often written with a capital letter, as Thermos, especially when it is the name of a brand.
fact: In science, the gap stops heat moving by conduction and convection, and the shiny inside reduces radiation.
exercise: What does a thermos do? | It makes drinks fizzy | It cools food in a freezer | *It keeps drinks hot or cold for a long time
exercise: «Arctic animals are built like thermos flasks.» What is the comparison about? | Eating green plants | Swimming fast in the sea | *Keeping warm in the cold | Singing loudly at night
exercise: Which is another name for a thermos? | *Vacuum flask | Teapot | Lunch box | Kettle
test: In «a thermos of hot chocolate», what is a thermos? | *A bottle that keeps the drink hot | A type of coat | A small plate | A tin for biscuits
test: Why does a thermos have a gap between its two walls? | To let air in for the tea | To make it look bigger | To hold more sugar | *To stop heat moving in or out
exam: Which sentence uses thermos correctly? | The thermos swam across the pond | Dad mended the thermos with a hammer | We ate a thermos for lunch | *Ella put a thermos of soup in her bag for the walk
